module bindings.ruby.nested_param_expansion_without_depth_limit;

// A nested-parameter decoder splits a query string into pairs,
// percent-decodes each key, and then expands the bracket groups in the key
// into nested containers: `a[b][c]=v` becomes {"a" => {"b" => {"c" => v}}}.
// The bracket groups are caller-written, so one container is allocated per
// group and the finished structure is walked one level at a time; with no
// ceiling on how many groups a key may carry, the depth of the expansion is a
// property of the request, and a key like a[b][c][d]... exhausts the stack or
// the heap one level per pair. Rack's QueryParser sets this convention's
// ceiling and names it in its public interface -- `param_depth_limit`, which
// raises ParamsTooDeepError once nesting reaches it -- and faraday's
// NestedParamsEncoder documents itself as the same convention ("this is not
// compatible with Rack::Utils.parse_nested_query"), so the convention's two
// implementations spell the control identically.
//
// The four positive conjuncts are the convention's own surface, co-occurring
// in one module: an `unescape` call (percent-decoding a pair), an exact '&'
// string literal (the pair separator the query string is split on), and a
// `scan` whose pattern contains the bracket pair (the nesting delimiter),
// which is how a decoder breaks `a[b][c]` into subkeys. Across every Ruby
// file in the corpus clones (24659 files) that co-occurrence holds only in
// faraday's own encoder; the next-nearest file, ruby/uri's rfc2396_parser,
// carries the separator only inside comments, and comments produce no
// literal token.
//
// The negation names the ceiling in the spellings the convention uses --
// `param_depth_limit` first, then the generic depth/nesting/recursion ceiling
// spellings -- and reads only the AST-derived `assign:` and `expr:` tokens. A
// live ceiling is either configured (an assignment) or compared against (a
// binary), and both survive comment stripping, while the module context also
// carries the module's raw source text, which a `literal:` or whole-token
// clause would let a comment silence. `dependency(...)` is deliberately
// absent: Ruby projects declare dependencies in the Gemfile, which is not a
// parsed manifest, so the gate would make the binding inert on every real
// application; the parsing conjuncts carry the technology instead.
//
// Residuals: a decoder that slices the key instead of scanning it (rack's own
// spelling of the expansion) keeps the weakness and is not reported, and an
// accessor declared but never assigned or compared (`attr_accessor
// :param_depth_limit` alone) does not silence, because symbols emit no
// tokens -- a ceiling declared but never set is nil at run time and enforces
// nothing. The module context is the file root, so one flag covers a file
// however the decoder is nested.

binding nestedParamExpansionWithoutDepthLimit {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "module"
    and node.context.language == "ruby"
    and node.context.call in ["unescape"]
    and node.context.call in ["scan"]
    and containsAny(node.context.regex, ["\\[\\]"])
    and node.context.literal in ["&"]
    and not containsAny(node.context.assign, ["param_depth_limit", "depth_limit", "depthLimit", "max_depth", "maxDepth", "max_nesting", "maxNesting", "nesting_depth", "nestingDepth", "nesting_limit", "nestingLimit", "recursion_depth", "recursionDepth", "recursion_limit", "recursionLimit"])
    and not containsAny(node.context.expr, ["param_depth_limit", "depth_limit", "depthLimit", "max_depth", "maxDepth", "max_nesting", "maxNesting", "nesting_depth", "nestingDepth", "nesting_limit", "nestingLimit", "recursion_depth", "recursionDepth", "recursion_limit", "recursionLimit"])
  emit issue code.NestedParamExpansionWithoutDepthLimit at node
}
